#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163289 :tutorial/content "For discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$, the **conditional expected value of $X$ given $Y=y$** is defined by\n\n$$\n\\textrm E\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y=y\\end{bmatrix}=\\sum_{x}x f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid y)$ is the conditional probability mass function of $X$ given $Y$.\n\nThis definition is similar to that of $\\textrm{E}[X]$. However, we now use the conditional PMF $f_{X\\mid Y}(x \\mid y)$ instead of the marginal PMF $f_X(x)$ in the summation.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The conditional expectation $\\textrm E\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y = y\\end{bmatrix}$ is (in general) a function of $y$. This makes intuitive sense. If the distribution of $X$ varies with $Y$, then the expected value of $X$ for a given $Y$ must vary with the value of $Y$.\n- If $y$ is replaced with a number, then $\\textrm E\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y = y\\end{bmatrix}$ returns a single value.\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then $\\textrm E\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y = y\\end{bmatrix} = \\textrm E[X]$ for all $y$. Again, this makes intuitive sense. If the outcome of $Y$ has no influence on the outcome of $X$, then the distribution of $X$ and, consequently, its mean, is also unaffected by $Y$.\n- We sometimes use the notation $\\mu_{X \\mid y}$ to denote $E\\begin{bmatrix}X & Y = y\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nFinally, we have an analogous definition for\n\n$$\n\\textrm E\\begin{bmatrix}Y & X=x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\textrm E\\begin{bmatrix}Y & X=x\\end{bmatrix}=\\sum_{y}y f_{Y\\mid X}(y \\mid x)\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "952ea536-2bdb-5613-b927-e32a2c37ab95" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5617 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
